      "text": "The rooftop pigeon coop above Mr. Aziz's tailor shop had been locked from the inside for four days, which was impossible, since the only door to it was on the outside.\n\nRashed noticed the discrepancy the way he noticed everything — through numbers. Mr. Aziz's shop ledger, which Rashed balanced every Thursday evening for a small fee, showed a delivery of fabric that had arrived but never been logged as sold, paid for, or stored. Four days' worth of a very precise, very small amount of money moving in a pattern that made no sense for a tailor shop.\n\n'Probably nothing,' Mr. Aziz said, when Rashed asked about it, too quickly for a man who usually loved explaining his business in exhausting detail.\n\nRashed climbed to the roof that evening under the excuse of retrieving a kite that had never actually landed there. The coop door was indeed locked, but through the gap beneath it, he could see fresh footprints in the dust — and the faint, unmistakable smell of expensive cigarette smoke, the kind none of the pigeon-keepers on this street could afford.\n\nSomeone had been living up there. And Mr. Aziz, judging by the fear behind his too-quick answer, knew exactly who."
