      "cover": "storyAssets/rashed/ep2.jpg",
      "text": "It took Rashed two days of quiet ledger-checking across six shops to find the pattern: small, identical payments moving from four different businesses into an account he didn't recognize, always on the same day of the week, always rounded to suspiciously clean numbers.\n\nHe wrote it all out on a napkin at the tea stall, the way he always worked through a problem, and that's when Nazma — who ran the stall and saw more of the street's business than anyone admitted — leaned over his shoulder.\n\n'You're looking at the protection money,' she said, so plainly that Rashed nearly dropped his pen. 'Everyone pays it. You don't, because nobody thinks a bookkeeper's worth the trouble.'\n\n'Paid to who?'\n\nNazma's expression shifted, the easy gossip draining out of it. 'The man who's been sleeping on Aziz's roof. Calls himself a landlord, like that word means something different up there than it does down here.' She tapped the napkin. 'You should stop adding those numbers up, Rashed. Some totals aren't meant to be solved. They're meant to be paid.'\n\nBut Rashed had never been able to leave a column unbalanced in his life, and he wasn't about to start now."
